    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0391" n="275" xml:id="pg275"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.55/30.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chinese Minister.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 74.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 25, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In further reply to your note of May 13,
                    1915, requesting that the American Minister at Habana be instructed to cooperate
                    with the Chinese Chargé d’Affaires there in an effort to prevail upon the Cuban
                    Government to continue in force, pending the conclusion of a treaty between
                    China and Cuba, the order suspending for sixty days the prohibition against
                    Chinese immigration into Cuba, I have the honor to enclose herewith for your
                    information and guidance, paraphrase of a telegram received by the Department
                    May 22 from the American Minister at Habana relative to this matter.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
